Route What it does <code>GET /health</code> <code>{"ok": true, "strategy": "&lt;name&gt;"}</code> <code>GET /strategy</code> The <code>StrategyInfo</code>: name, description, resources, metadata. <code>GET /resources</code> Every resource. <code>GET /packages</code>, <code>GET /tactics</code>, <code>GET /channels</code> Resources of one kind. <code>POST /run</code> Call the runner. <code>POST /resources/{name}/invoke</code> Call any resource: run its tactic or handler, or use its store channel. <code>GET /channels/{name}/events</code> Query a channel resource: <code>?after_cursor=0&amp;limit=100&amp;kind=...</code>. <code>POST /channels/{name}/events</code> Append to a channel resource. The <code>input</code> is an object of event fields: <code>payload</code>, <code>kind</code> (default <code>event</code>), <code>source</code> (default <code>lllm.shell</code>), <code>schema</code>, <code>metadata</code>, <code>correlation_id</code>, <code>parent_ids</code>. <code>/tactics/{name}/...</code> The mounted tactic service: <code>POST run</code>, <code>POST stream</code>, <code>GET info</code>, custom endpoints. <code>/network/...</code> The store API, when the strategy has a store. <p>The shell routes (<code>/run</code>, <code>/resources/...</code>, <code>/channels/...</code>) take a <code>StrategyRunRequest</code>, <code>{"input": ..., "context": {...}}</code> (the <code>GET</code> channel route takes query parameters), and return a <code>StrategyRunResponse</code>, <code>{"output": ..., "strategy": ..., "metadata": {...}}</code>:</p> <pre><code>response = client.post(
    "/resources/inbox/invoke",
    json={"input": {"op": "info"}, "context": {"request": "demo"}},
)
print(response.json())
</code></pre> <pre><code>{'output': {'name': 'inbox', 'schema': None, 'form': 'log', 'description': 'Support messages as they arrive.', 'metadata': {}}, 'strategy': 'support-desk', 'metadata': {'resource': 'inbox', 'endpoint': 'resource'}}
</code></pre> <p>A tactic resource runs the same way on <code>/resources/{name}/invoke</code> as on <code>/tactics/{name}/run</code>: <code>context</code> has the shape of a <code>CallContext</code> (<code>request_id</code>, <code>trace_id</code>, <code>caller</code>, <code>tags</code>, <code>metadata</code>, and the other fields; keys that are not <code>CallContext</code> fields are dropped), the tactic receives a <code>CallContext</code>, and failures get the same status codes. Only the response differs: the <code>/tactics/{name}/</code> routes return the services envelope, <code>{"output": ..., "request_id": ..., "tactic": ...}</code>. The runner and the handlers of other resources get <code>context</code> as a plain dict; a <code>Tactic</code> used as such a handler receives it as <code>CallContext.metadata</code>. <code>/resources/{name}/invoke</code> with a store-backed channel takes an <code>op</code>: <code>append</code>, <code>query</code> (the default), or <code>info</code>, plus that op's fields.</p> <p>The shell routes report errors in the services error envelope, as the tactic routes do:</p> <pre><code>response = client.get("/channels/triage/events")
print(response.status_code, response.json())
</code></pre> <pre><code>404 {'detail': {'error': {'type': 'ResourceNotFoundError', 'message': 'Strategy channel not found: triage', 'tactic': None, 'endpoint': 'channel', 'request_id': None, 'metadata': {'resource': 'triage'}}}}
</code></pre> Status When 400 A malformed request, an appended event whose <code>input</code> is not an object, or a <code>ValueError</code>, <code>SchemaError</code>, or <code>TacticUnsupportedError</code> from the runner or a handler. 404 The resource named in the URL does not exist, or a <code>/channels/</code> route names a resource that is not a channel. Its own An <code>HTTPException</code> from the runner or a handler keeps its status and its plain <code>detail</code>, without the envelope; a store error keeps the store API's code. 500 Anything else the runner or a handler raises, including a resource lookup that fails inside it. <p>A tactic resource fails with the codes of <code>POST /tactics/{name}/run</code> instead, on both routes: 400 for <code>SchemaError</code> and <code>TacticUnsupportedError</code>, 500 for anything else, a <code>ValueError</code>, <code>HTTPException</code>, or store error included. The store API at <code>/network/</code> uses the same envelope with only <code>type</code> and <code>message</code>.</p>
